<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8' standalone='yes' ?>
<!--
    Carrier config defaults for this device, applied to EVERY carrier.

    DefaultCarrierConfigService reads this file as a RESOURCE
    (DefaultCarrierConfigService.java:130, `getResources().getXml(R.xml.vendor)`),
    not as an asset, which is why a device resource overlay reaches it and no
    upstream change is needed. It is merged last, over whatever
    assets/carrier_config_*.xml matched, so a value set here wins for every SIM.
    AOSP's own copy of this file is an empty <carrier_config_list/>, so nothing
    is lost by replacing it.

    The `device` filter is redundant, because this overlay only exists in this
    product's image, but it states the scope at the point of the change and
    costs nothing. It is matched against Build.DEVICE
    (DefaultCarrierConfigService.checkFilters()).
-->
<carrier_config_list>
    <carrier_config device="k50sv1_64_bsp">
        <!--
            Do NOT route emergency calls to IMS first.

            AOSP defaults this true (CarrierConfigManager.java:3305; the key
            itself is declared at :722-723). With it
            true, GsmCdmaPhone.dial() takes the IMS path for ANY emergency call
            whenever `imsPhone.isImsAvailable()` holds, and that predicate asks
            only whether the ImsService BINDER is alive (ImsPhone.java:449 to
            ImsPhoneCallTracker.java:884 to ImsManager.java:1630), never
            whether IMS is REGISTERED. Note the asymmetry with the
            ordinary-call predicate immediately above it at
            GsmCdmaPhone.java:1180: that one does require
            getServiceState() == STATE_IN_SERVICE. The emergency one does not.

            This device binds MediaTek's MMTEL service (config_dynamic_bind_ims
            plus config_ims_package in overlay/frameworks/.../config.xml), so
            the binder is always alive and every emergency dial went to IMS
            even with IMS unregistered. It then wedged, permanently:

              ImsCallSessionProxy: [callId = null] terminate() :
                  Terminate Call fail since there is no call ID. Abnormal Case

            MTK's blob logs that and returns WITHOUT throwing ImsException, so
            ImsPhoneCallTracker.hangup() (:1720-1777) neither reaches its local
            cleanup branch (:1766, unreachable because imsCall != null) nor its
            catch (:1775, never entered), and the 500 ms EVENT_HANGUP_PENDINGMO
            safety net is never scheduled. The connection stays in DIALING
            forever, and CallsManager.java:3413-3431 then refuses every LATER
            emergency call. Recovery needed a phone-process kill.

            Turning this off avoids the confirmed IMS-null-call wedge and sends
            the request down the CS path. It does NOT guarantee completion: CS
            dial, hangup and radio-power requests share the same MTK AT channel,
            which has also wedged for five minutes. Nothing is lost while IMS
            is unregistered, and this key is the supported route selector. AOSP
            itself ships it false for Jazztel
            (assets/carrier_config_carrierid_1974_Jazztel.xml:4).

            REVISIT if VoLTE ever registers on a SIM with service here: IMS
            E911 is preferable where it actually works. See WI-029.
        -->
        <boolean name="carrier_use_ims_first_for_emergency_bool" value="false" />

        <!--
            VoLTE is a capability of this device, for every SIM.

            ImsManager.isVolteEnabledByPlatform() (ImsManager.java:622-637) ANDs
            config_device_volte_available, which this device's framework overlay
            sets true, with KEY_CARRIER_VOLTE_AVAILABLE_BOOL, which
            CarrierConfigManager.java:3278 defaults to FALSE and which only the
            ~24 carrier assets AOSP ships ever set true. None of them matches
            the operators this handset sees, so without this line VoLTE is off
            on every SIM regardless of network or user setting.

            The previous revision solved that with persist.dbg.volte_avail_ovr=1.
            That property is a debug short-circuit: it returns true before the
            AND is evaluated, so it also bypassed config_device_volte_available
            and isGbaValid(), and it did nothing for the other readers of
            KEY_CARRIER_VOLTE_AVAILABLE_BOOL. Setting the key here is the
            supported route and keeps all three gates real.

            This does NOT claim the operator supports VoLTE. It says the device
            does. Whether the network registers IMS is still decided by the
            network, by CarrierConfig provisioning keys (all left at their AOSP
            defaults, which do not require provisioning), and by the user's
            Enhanced 4G LTE toggle.
        -->
        <boolean name="carrier_volte_available_bool" value="true" />

        <!--
            Wi-Fi calling is a capability of this device, for every SIM.

            Exactly the same gate shape as VoLTE above:
            ImsManager.isWfcEnabledByPlatform() ANDs
            config_device_wfc_ims_available (set true in this device's framework
            overlay) with KEY_CARRIER_WFC_IMS_AVAILABLE_BOOL, which
            CarrierConfigManager defaults to FALSE and which only AOSP's handful
            of carrier assets ever set true. None of them matches the operators
            this handset sees.

            This does NOT claim the operator provisions VoWiFi. It says the
            device can do it. Whether an ePDG accepts the UE is decided by the
            network, and the residual risk is named in
            work/k50sv1-bringup/notes/vowifi-feasibility.md: this SIM has no
            ISIM application (IccCardStatus reports ims_id=-1), so the IMPI
            WifiOffloadService hands MAL is empty and epdg_wod has to derive the
            IDi itself.

            Every WFC provisioning/mode key is left at its AOSP default:
            KEY_CARRIER_VOLTE_PROVISIONING_REQUIRED_BOOL is false, and
            KEY_CARRIER_DEFAULT_WFC_IMS_MODE_INT defaults to WIFI_PREFERRED.
            Nothing here forces a mode; the user's setting decides.
        -->
        <boolean name="carrier_wfc_ims_available_bool" value="true" />
    </carrier_config>
</carrier_config_list>
